
Given a binary tree, find the lowest common ancestor (LCA) of two given nodes in the tree.

According to the definition of LCA on Wikipedia: “The lowest common ancestor is defined between two nodes p and q as the lowest node in T that has both p and q as descendants (where we allow a node to be a descendant of itself).”

  

Example 1:
        3
      /   \
     5     1
    / \   / \
   6   2 0   8
      / \
     7   4
LCA of 5 and 1 = 3
LCA of 5 and 4 = 5

Input: root = [3,5,1,6,2,0,8,null,null,7,4], p = 5, q = 1
Output: 3

Algorithm LCA(root, p, q)

1. If root is NULL
      return NULL

2. If root == p OR root == q
      return root

3. Recursively search the left subtree
      left = LCA(root.left, p, q)

4. Recursively search the right subtree
      right = LCA(root.right, p, q)

5. If both left and right are NOT NULL
      return root
      // One node found in left subtree
      // One node found in right subtree
      // Current node is the Lowest Common Ancestor

6. If left is NOT NULL
      return left
      // Both nodes are in the left subtree
      // or LCA already found in the left subtree

7. Otherwise
      return right
      // Both nodes are in the right subtree
      // or LCA already found in the right subtree


/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * class TreeNode(var `val`: Int = 0) {
 *     var left: TreeNode? = null
 *     var right: TreeNode? = null
 * }
 */

class Solution {
    fun lowestCommonAncestor(root: TreeNode?, p: TreeNode?, q: TreeNode?): TreeNode? {
        if(root == null || root == p || root == q) return root
        // find left LCA
        val leftLCA = lowestCommonAncestor(root.left,p,q)
        // find right LCA
        val rightLCA = lowestCommonAncestor(root.right,p,q)

        if(leftLCA != null && rightLCA != null) return root

        return leftLCA ?: rightLCA
    }
}
